  Performance:
    Shader Options:
      ☐ Replace user-configuration feature branches with declared precompiled shader options.
        - Use the existing typed ShaderOption/ShaderUse contracts, variant catalog and preparation
          lifecycle. Select the requested executable before pass/PSO preparation; reject a failed
          switch rather than rendering the previous policy. Avoid redundant variant combinations.
        - Keep numerical tuning values as uniforms where appropriate. Compile enable/disable choices
          separately, including features disabled by a zero-valued control; do not generate a variant
          for every possible amplitude, threshold or other continuous value.
        - Preserve runtime guards for geometry, lighting, current publications, resource availability,
          disocclusion and other per-frame/per-sample facts; these are not configuration switches.
        ☐ Compile cloud detail and its noise bypass as feature selections.
          - Replace volumeDetail.w and the zero-amplitude noise selection in cloud_volume_density.glsl
            with options derived from Clouds.Detail and whether DetailAmplitude is positive.
          - Apply the same density policy to reduced tracing, reference transport, retained secondary
            marching and shadow-cache generation. Preserve cubic support, connected faces, anchoring
            and actual amplitude/scale controls; omit disabled noise/erosion work from the binary.
        ☐ Compile cloud primary early-termination enablement separately from its threshold.
          - Derive enablement from Clouds.StopTransmittance > 0. Remove the per-step termination
            comparison when zero; retain the configured threshold and strict post-step exit when on.
          - Preserve primary sampling, support metadata, physical units and all OIT output arithmetic.
        ☐ Compile LumOn anchor jitter enablement in anchor and temporal shaders.
          - Replace the AnchorJitterEnabled uniform switch in lumon_probe_anchor.fsh and
            lumon_probe_atlas_temporal.fsh with a shared option; retain jitter scale/cycle as data.
          - Keep anchor coordinates and temporal reconstruction consistent across selection changes,
            including applicable history invalidation and debug consumers.
        ☐ Compile LumOn optional velocity-based reprojection.
          - Replace configuration-driven enableVelocityReprojection branches in the atlas temporal
            shader with an option derived from LumOn.EnableReprojectionVelocity.
          - Preserve displaced-surface rejection and other required geometry/history safety checks
            even when coordinate remapping is off. Retain velocity metadata/resources still needed
            for those checks; do not remove them merely because reprojection is disabled.
        ☐ Compile native FXAA enablement in luminance preparation and final display.
          - Replace the user-setting paths through passInfo.z and exposure.z in pbr_post_luma.fsh and
            pbr_final.fsh with coordinated options; exclude the edge filter and its luma preparation
            when disabled. Cover both local-exposure selections without changing display encoding.
          - Distinguish the persistent native FXAA setting from the transient postprocessing pass gate.
        ☐ Compile disabled bloom and light-shaft radiance out of final composition.
          - Select bloom/shaft consumer options from the configured effect enablement, alongside the
            existing CPU producer gating. Exclude disabled texture sampling, arithmetic and sampler
            requirements from pbr_final.fsh instead of always reading neutral replacement textures.
          - Preserve enabled effect composition order, source-local exposure, current-frame validity
            fallback and independent light-shaft occlusion consumers; do not conflate radiance with
            occlusion or remove guards for unavailable publications.
        - Verify each enabled/disabled binary structurally through emitted SPIR-V/reflection and lean
          correctness checks using existing typed GPU fixtures. Run build/test tools through subagents;
          avoid redundant matrices, approximation-quality gates and unmeasured performance claims.
          Measure representative costs separately and reconcile current-state shader documentation.
